      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50044919",
      "why_read": "Read this to discover how SQLite can handle concurrent writes across multiple processes without modifying its core code. You will learn the history of SQLite write bottlenecks and the technical trade-offs of previous workarounds.",
      "authors": [
        "Marco Bambini"
      ],
      "image": "/infographics/03-hn-50044919.jpg",
      "content": "SQLite powers trillions of deployments, yet every backend engineer hits the exact same wall: the single-writer lock. Whether through write-ahead logging or busy timeouts, the engine fundamentally permits only one process to hold the write lock at any given moment.\n\nA new architectural approach tackles concurrent writes without modifying a single line of SQLite source code. Instead of replacing the storage layer or rewriting the engine with MVCC like Turso did, this system coordinates multi-process writes before they collide with SQLite's internal locking mechanisms. It preserves complete compatibility with standard database files while enabling concurrent writes across processes.\n\nBypassing the traditional write serialization bottleneck opens up massive headroom for read-heavy and mixed workloads running directly on local storage.\n\nLocal embedded databases do not have to choose between simplicity and multi-core throughput."
    },
