      "utility_score": 8,
      "novelty_score": 7,
      "hn_id": "50048857",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50048857",
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the mechanistic trade-offs of query pushdown when bridging Postgres and ClickHouse. You will learn how foreign data wrappers balance remote query execution against raw row transfer.",
      "authors": [
        "Kaushik Iska",
        "David Wheeler",
        "Philip Dub\\xE9"
      ],
      "content": "Building a Postgres Foreign Data Wrapper to query an external analytics engine like ClickHouse sounds conceptually straightforward until query pushdown enters the picture. The core challenge is not establishing network connections, but deciding how much computation to delegate across the wire versus pulling raw rows back into the local engine.\n\nWhen Postgres plans a query against an external foreign table, pushdown behaves like a delicate negotiation. Passing clauses such as WHERE, GROUP BY, or LIMIT directly to ClickHouse minimizes network transfer. However, semantic differences in functions, type coercion, and dialect subtleties mean that full pushdown can produce silent inconsistencies or invalid SQL.\n\nThe engineering team at ClickHouse found that successful pushdown requires three distinct strategies: direct delegation for native operations, on-the-fly SQL AST rewrites for partial mismatches, and intentional fallbacks to local execution when safe translation cannot be guaranteed.\n\nPushing work down is not an all-or-nothing switch; it is a fine-grained dialect translation problem."
    },
    {
      "title": "Common replication assumptions underestimate data loss in large clusters",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://martin.kleppmann.com/2017/01/26/data-loss-in-large-clusters.html",
      "date": "2026-10-11",
      "tags": [
        "catchup",
        "data-durability",
        "disk-failure",
        "distributed-storage",
        "hn",
        "jbod",
